---
// GDPR (EU 2016/679) privacy page draft. Before launch:
//   1. Fill every [BRACKET] slot. The controller is whoever legally runs the site.
//      Then delete '/privacy' from UNFILLED_UNTIL_LAUNCH in
//      tests/placeholders.spec.ts (the test turns red until you do).
//   2. Make section 3 match reality. The starter ships with analytics off
//      (USE_ANALYTICS = false in src/config.ts), but this page still ships the
//      Plausible paragraphs. A site without analytics deletes them (then also
//      trim the meta description below) and leaves the switch off. A site with
//      Plausible keeps them, picks the self-hosted OR cloud wording, and switches
//      analytics on: USE_ANALYTICS = true in src/config.ts and the analytics host
//      added to public/_headers (the analytics comment in src/config.ts says
//      how; in server mode the host stays out of _headers, since no script loads).
//      Keeping the text does not start the script. Section 3 has
//      slots to settle: the hosting choice (self-hosted: first confirm that your
//      web server, reverse proxy or CDN keeps no IP addresses in access logs for
//      the measurement host, or say so here); one sentence each for outbound links,
//      file downloads and form submissions (keep a sentence only if that
//      measurement is ticked in Plausible's installer, which ticks all three by
//      default; the page then describes what the instance does); the storage
//      period; and the analytics host in the sentence on refusing it. The
//      sentence on clicks on an email address describes the "Email Tap" event
//      (src/components/EmailLink.astro, and the button of the
//      website-contact-form skill's form); it goes with the paragraph, and stays
//      as long as that counting does. The clause "only a visit that is still going ... continues as the
//      same visit" describes Plausible's own session lookup (self-hosted Community
//      Edition, checked for v3.2.1: on_event and find_session in
//      lib/plausible/session/cache_store.ex, register_session in
//      lib/plausible/ingestion/event.ex, salts.ex, and the RotateSalts cron in
//      config/runtime.exs). With Plausible Cloud keep it only if Plausible confirms
//      the same; with another analytics tool delete it together with the paragraph.
//      Analytics mode (ANALYTICS.mode in src/config.ts): in script mode (the default)
//      delete every [SERVER MODE ONLY …] slot and keep the text of the [SCRIPT MODE
//      ONLY …] ones; in server mode the other way round, and delete the three
//      optional-measurement slot sentences too (no script runs, so none of them
//      happens). Section 2 has one server-mode sentence.
//      The sentence on how to refuse the measurement has a slot for the host
//      name: name the same host as ANALYTICS.scriptHost in src/config.ts
//      (plausible.io for Plausible Cloud). If that host is the site's own domain
//      (Plausible behind a proxy), blocking it also blocks the site's own
//      scripts: name the script's full address there instead. Keep the host inside
//      the span marked data-analytics-host: tests/privacy-host.spec.ts compares it
//      with ANALYTICS.scriptHost once the slot is filled. Switzerland's
//      Telecommunications Act (Art. 45c FMG) asks that visitors are told about
//      the processing and its purpose, and that they may refuse it. The "Do Not
//      Track" paragraph near the top of section 3 stays even without analytics:
//      California's CalOPPA asks the policy to say whether third parties may
//      collect personal information across websites through the site, and how the
//      site answers "Do Not Track" if it collects such information itself.
//      In server mode that refusal sentence is deleted with the other [SCRIPT MODE
//      ONLY …] slots, mark and all (no script loads, so there is nothing to block), and
//      its server-mode slot says so.
//   3. German-market sites: German law expects legal pages in German. German-ONLY
//      site → swap this page for the vetted German draft that ships in this
//      repo at src/pages/_datenschutz.astro (underscore = unrouted until you
//      rename it; its header says how; it serves at /datenschutz and REPLACES
//      this page — never ship both). Multilingual site via astro-i18n-setup →
//      the German privacy page lives at the locale-prefixed route (/de/privacy)
//      instead; use the German draft as its content.
//   4. Consent law: this page states no consent conclusion on purpose. Running
//      without a banner is a position, not a settled fact. The script sets no
//      cookies and writes nothing to the device. It sends the page address and the
//      referrer, scroll depth and engaged time (and, if ticked in Plausible, the
//      address of clicked outbound links and downloaded files), and one "Email Tap"
//      event for each click on an email address or the contact form's button, with
//      the page address and the referrer as every Plausible event has them, and
//      neither the address nor anything typed (a custom event, so wider than bare
//      counting). It also reads one
//      local-storage flag (plausible_ignore, which lets you exclude your own
//      visits) and navigator.webdriver (a bot check); neither is sent. The EDPB
//      (Guidelines 2/2023 v2.0, para 33) says JavaScript that instructs the browser
//      to send information "clearly falls within the scope of Article 5(3)"; para
//      53 treats information that client-side code sends back to a server as a
//      gaining of access; para 56 adds that this does not by itself make consent
//      necessary. § 25 TDDDG (formerly TTDSG) has two exceptions in Abs. 2 and none
//      for statistics. The DSK (OH Digitale Dienste v1.2, Nov 2024) makes no
//      statement that reach measurement is consent-free (Rz. 87 to 89), at most for
//      a precisely defined configuration that stops holding when more information
//      is added, and says even simple counting is not automatically part of the
//      basic service (Rz. 90). Plausible's own legal assessment concludes from
//      "does not store any information on the user's device" and does not discuss
//      the script reading the page address or the referrer. This tag measures more
//      than bare counting. Other EU markets have their own ePrivacy transpositions.
//      Have the position reviewed for your market and record who accepted it.
//      Server mode changes the facts above, not the need for a review: no script runs,
//      nothing is read from or written to the device, and the browser sends only the
//      page request it sends anyway; Cloudflare's Function passes the page address,
//      the referrer, the user agent and the IP address on to Plausible. Paras 33 and
//      53 then no longer describe the measurement. But the same guidelines (paras 54
//      and 55) say that tracking based on the IP address alone can fall under Article
//      5(3) when the address comes from the visitor's device, and that unless the site
//      can ensure it does not, it has to take the Article 5(3) steps; para 56 again
//      says this does not by itself make consent necessary. Plausible still uses the
//      IP address and the user agent to tell visitors apart for a day. And a visitor
//      can no longer opt out by blocking a script. Record who reviewed this too.
//      ANY third-party embed (YouTube iframe, remote Google Fonts), another
//      analytics tool, or wider measurement (optional measurements such as outbound
//      links, file downloads or form submissions; custom properties) needs this
//      position AND the text re-checked BEFORE you ship such an addition; a
//      third-party embed or a cross-site tracker also makes the "Do Not Track"
//      paragraph untrue. Base.astro also reads a 'theme' key from local storage,
//      and nothing in the template writes it. If you add a toggle that stores the
//      choice, say so in section 3.
//   5. A solid baseline, not legal advice. When in doubt, have it reviewed.
// The copy below already passes the a11y / seo / tone / email suites: keep one
// <h1>, the description in the 120-160 char window, no em dashes or banned
// contractions, and every email address behind <EmailLink>.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import EmailLink from '../components/EmailLink.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config';
---
<Base
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description={`How ${SITE.name} handles personal data: cookieless analytics, hosting on Cloudflare Pages, contact data handling, and your rights under the GDPR.`}>
  <section class="wrap">
    <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p>
      Last updated: [DATE]. This page explains which personal data this website
      processes, on what legal basis under the GDPR (EU 2016/679), and which
      rights you have.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Controller</h2>
    <p>Responsible for data processing on this website:</p>
    <p>
      [CONTROLLER LEGAL NAME]<br />
      [STREET AND NUMBER]<br />
      [POSTCODE, CITY, COUNTRY]<br />
      Contact: <EmailLink address="hello@example.com" subject="Privacy request" />
    </p>

    <h2>2. Hosting and server logs</h2>
    <p>
      This site is served by <strong>Cloudflare Pages</strong> (Cloudflare, Inc.,
      101 Townsend St, San Francisco, CA 94107, USA). When you visit a page,
      Cloudflare processes technical connection data (IP address, date and time,
      requested URL, referring page, browser user agent) to deliver the site, to
      protect it against attacks, and to give us traffic statistics (for example
      requests, visitors and their countries). The legal basis is our legitimate
      interest in operating a secure and fast website and in knowing how much it
      is used (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR). Cloudflare acts as our
      processor under Art. 28 GDPR; transfers to the USA are covered by the
      EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework and standard contractual clauses. We do not
      combine these logs with any other data.
      [SERVER MODE ONLY, DELETE IN SCRIPT MODE: On this site's own domain,
      Cloudflare also passes the data described in section 3 to our analytics
      tool.]
    </p>

    <h2>3. Cookies and analytics</h2>
    <p>
      This website is ad-free and <strong>sets no cookies</strong>. Your visit
      leaves no advertising or cross-site tracking identifiers with us.
    </p>
    <p>
      Your browser may send a "Do Not Track" or "Global Privacy Control"
      signal. This site handles every visit the same way, with or without it:
      it does not follow you across other websites. The service providers named
      on this page process data for us as described here. No other third party
      collects personal information about you over time and across websites
      through this site.
    </p>
    <p>
      To understand how the site is used, we run <strong>Plausible
      Analytics</strong>, a web analytics tool
      [CHOOSE ONE: self-hosted on our own server (first confirm that no web server,
      proxy or CDN log keeps IP addresses, or say so here) / operated for us as our processor
      (Art. 28 GDPR) by Plausible Insights O&Uuml;, V&auml;striku tn 2, 50403 Tartu,
      Estonia (EU)].
      [SCRIPT MODE ONLY, DELETE IN SERVER MODE: Its script sets no cookies and writes nothing to your
      device. It checks one setting in your browser, which lets you exclude your
      own visits, and whether the browser is automated; neither is sent to the
      measurement server. When you open a page, it sends the address of that page and the
      page or site you came from to the measurement server, together with the
      technical data your browser sends with every request (browser, operating
      system, device type, IP address). When you leave the page or switch to
      another tab or window, it also sends
      how far you scrolled and how long the page was visible and in focus.]
      [SERVER MODE ONLY, DELETE IN SCRIPT MODE: No analytics code runs in your
      browser, and nothing is stored on or read from your device for it. When
      you open a page, the server that delivers this site (Cloudflare, see
      section 2) passes the address of that page and the page or site you came
      from, if your browser sends it, to the measurement server, together with
      the technical data your browser sends with every request (browser,
      operating system, device type, IP address).]
      [DELETE UNLESS OUTBOUND LINKS IS TICKED IN PLAUSIBLE: It counts clicks on
      links to other websites and sends and stores the address of the link you clicked.]
      [DELETE UNLESS FILE DOWNLOADS IS TICKED IN PLAUSIBLE: It counts clicks on
      links to files, such as PDFs, and sends and stores the address of the file.]
      [DELETE UNLESS FORM SUBMISSIONS IS TICKED IN PLAUSIBLE: It counts
      submissions of forms on the site, but not what you enter.]
      [SCRIPT MODE ONLY, DELETE IN SERVER MODE: It also counts each click on an email address on this site, and on a
      button that opens your email program, together with the page it was on
      and the page or site you came from. Neither the email address nor
      anything you typed is sent.]
      [SERVER MODE ONLY, DELETE IN SCRIPT MODE: A click on an email address on
      this site passes through the server that delivers this site (Cloudflare),
      which counts it together with the
      page it was on and then opens your email program. The email address is
      not sent to the measurement server.]
      The tool derives your
      approximate location from the IP address and does not keep the IP
      address in its own records. From your IP address, your browser data
      and this website's domain,
      plus a value that is replaced every 24 hours, it forms an identifier.
      While that value lasts, and as long as those inputs stay the same, the
      tool therefore recognises the same browser and counts it as one
      visitor. Once the value is replaced, it can no longer recognise that
      browser; only a visit that is still going at that moment (one with
      activity in about the last 30 minutes) continues as the same visit. Nor
      can the tool recognise that browser on another website. The individual
      events (for example each page view[SCRIPT MODE ONLY, DELETE IN SERVER MODE: , and the scroll depth and engaged
      time sent when you leave a page or switch away from it]) and the
      session records are stored with this identifier, the page address, the
      referrer, the location and the device data, and are shown as
      aggregated counts, for example page views, referrers and countries.
    </p>
    <p>
      The legal basis is our legitimate interest in understanding how the
      website is used and in improving it (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR).
      [STATE THE STORAGE PERIOD, for example: We keep these records for 24
      months and then delete them.] [SCRIPT MODE ONLY, DELETE IN SERVER MODE: You can refuse the measurement: block
      scripts from <span data-analytics-host>[ANALYTICS HOST, the host name in
      ANALYTICS.scriptHost in src/config.ts, for example plausible.io]</span>
      with a content blocker in your browser. The site works the same without
      the measurement.] [SERVER MODE ONLY, DELETE IN SCRIPT
      MODE: The measurement happens on the server, so blocking scripts in your
      browser does not prevent it. You can object to it (see section 5).]
    </p>

    <h2>4. Contact by email</h2>
    <p>
      If you contact us, we process the data you provide (name, email address,
      and the content of your message) to handle the request and possible
      follow-up questions. The legal basis is Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR where the request
      relates to a contract, otherwise our legitimate interest in answering
      enquiries (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR). We keep the correspondence only as long as
      needed for this purpose and for any statutory retention duties.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      Under the GDPR you have the right of access (Art. 15), rectification
      (Art. 16), erasure (Art. 17), restriction of processing (Art. 18), data
      portability (Art. 20), and objection to processing based on legitimate
      interest (Art. 21). To exercise any of these rights, contact us via the
      address in section 1; we will verify your identity before acting on a
      request. You also have the right to lodge a complaint with a data
      protection supervisory authority (Art. 77 GDPR).
    </p>

    <h2>6. Changes to this page</h2>
    <p>
      We will update this page whenever the data processing on this site
      changes, and adjust the date at the top accordingly.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
